WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.047
Dangling pointer prevention is often an API and class design problem.

2
00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:10.670
A function or constructor that accepts a pointer, reference, view, or callback is also accepting a lifetime relationship.

3
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.349
The signature should make that relationship as obvious as practical.

4
00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:17.293
Avoid storing borrowed pointers or references by default.

5
00:00:17.293 --> 00:00:26.859
If a class stores an observer to an external object, the owner must outlive the observing class or the observer must have a way to verify

6
00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:27.963
availability before use.

7
00:00:27.963 --> 00:00:33.482
That relationship should be documented, tested, and visible in the member type or constructor contract.

8
00:00:33.482 --> 00:00:36.057
Member variables should distinguish ownership from observation.

9
00:00:36.057 --> 00:00:43.048
Values and RAII members own their state directly. std::unique_ptr expresses exclusive dynamic ownership. std::shared_ptr expresses shared ownership

10
00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:51.510
when the design truly requires it. std::weak_ptr, handles, identifiers, and raw non-owning observers can express observation when paired with a clear

11
00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:51.878
owner.

12
00:00:51.878 --> 00:00:58.501
Stable handles or registries can be useful when objects need identity across moves, container mutation, or subsystem boundaries.

13
00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:02.180
A handle should have defined validity rules and failure behavior.

14
00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.596
It should not merely hide a raw pointer behind a different name.

15
00:01:06.596 --> 00:01:08.803
Make lifetime assumptions testable and reviewable.

16
00:01:08.803 --> 00:01:18.369
A reader should be able to tell whether a function stores access beyond the call, whether a class owns its dependencies, and what happens when the

17
00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:19.473
owner goes away.

18
00:01:19.473 --> 00:01:25.360
When those answers are not visible, the design deserves another pass before implementation spreads the ambiguity.
